4.2 Greedy Algorithm
In this subsection we propose an algorithm for the FSC problem
whose approximation ratio does not depend on𝑘. For simplicity
of explanation, we describe our algorithm for the case we have
only two demographic groups, i.e., G={g1,g2}. In the end, our
algorithm can be extended almost verbatim to 𝑘>2groups.
Let𝑋=∅be the cover we construct and let 𝑈−=𝑈be the
set of uncovered elements. We repeat the following steps until
𝑈−=∅. In each iteration, we find the pair of sets(𝑆𝐴,𝑆𝐵)such that
i) sets𝑆𝐴,𝑆𝐵cover the maximum number of uncovered elements,
i.e.,𝑈−∩(𝑆𝐴∪𝑆𝐵)is maximized, and ii) set 𝑆𝐴has color g1and
𝑆𝐵has color g2, i.e., g(𝑆𝐴)=g1andg(𝑆𝐵)=g2. Once we find
𝑆𝐴,𝑆𝐵we update the set 𝑈−removing the new covered element,
𝑈−←𝑈−\(𝑆𝐴∪𝑆𝐵), and we add 𝑆𝐴,𝑆𝐵in𝑋. In the end, after
covering all elements ( 𝑈−=∅), we return 𝑋. The pseudocode of
our algorithm is shown in the technical report [14].
Analysis. First, it is straightforward to see that 𝑋is a fair cover.
The algorithm stops when there is no uncovered element so 𝑋is
a cover. In every iteration, we add exactly one set of color g1and
one set of color g2, so𝑋is a fair cover.
