7 Experiments
Having theoretically analyzed the proposed algorithms, in this
section we experimentally evaluate the efficiency and efficacy of
our algorithms on real and synthetic datasets.
Datasets: We use 3 real datasets, one semi-synthesized, and one
synthesized dataset to evaluate our methods. Our first set of ex-
periments (§ 7.1 and § 7.2) are motivated by Example 1 (Team of
Experts Formation), for which we use the Strategeion Resume
Skills dataset1. It consists of 1986 rows as candidates with differ-
ent skills. The skill of a candidate is shown as 218 binary columns
each corresponding to one skill. As an AI Ethics benchmark, the
dataset also contains demographic group information, including
the (binary) gender. The male-to-female ratio in this dataset is 0.8.
Motivated by the "Fair survey data collection" application demon-
strated in § 2, we use two additional real datasets: Adult Income
andCOMPAS (§ 7.3). The Adult Income Dataset [ 18] consists
of demographic information for individuals, which was extracted
from the 1994 Census database. The dataset is primarily used to
predict whether a person earns more than $50,000 per year based
on various attributes (14 columns and 48,842 rows). Gender is the
sensitive attribute in this dataset. The COMPAS dataset [ 1] con-
tains data used to evaluate the effectiveness and fairness of the
COMPAS recidivism prediction algorithm. This dataset includes
criminal history, jail and prison time, demographics, and COM-
PAS risk scores for defendants from Broward County, Florida (52
columns and 18,316 rows), while race is the sensitive attribute.
The third set of experiments (§ 7.4) are using the POPSIM [38]
dataset. POPSIM is a semi-synthetic dataset that combines popu-
lation statistics along with a geo-database. It is used to represent
1http://bit.ly/2SeS4xoindividual-level data with demographic information for the state of
Illinois. Race is used as the sensitive attribute (5 groups). It contains
2,000,000 locations represented as points in R2.
For the last set of experiments, (§ 7.5), we synthesized a dataset
consisting of a number of points as ground set and a family of sub-
sets with different coverage distributions on this ground set. More
details about this dataset is provided in the technical report [14].
Algorithms: We compare four algorithms over all the experi-
ments: Standard Greedy Set Cover (SC), Naive Algorithm (Naive),
Fair Greedy Algorithm (AllPick), Efficient or Faster Fair Greedy
Algorithm (EffAllPick). We also compare these algorithms with
the optimum solution (using the brute-force algorithm for Standard
Set Cover (Opt-SC) and a brute-force algorithm for Fair Set Cover
(Opt-Fair)). These algorithms simply check all possible covers to
find the best optimum solution. For the generalized version of the
problem, we compared the General Fair Set Cover (GFSC) algo-
rithm and the Efficient or Faster GFSC algorithm (EffGFSC) along
with Naive and Standard Greedy (SC) algorithms.
Evaluation metrics: We used three metrics for evaluating the
algorithms: (a) Output Cover Size, (b) Fairness Ratio, and (c)
Running Time. Following our (general) fairness definition in § 3,
let the ratio of each group gℎ∈ G in the cover 𝑋be𝑅(gℎ)=
1
𝑓ℎ|︁|︁{𝑋∩Sℎ}|︁|︁. Then, the Fairness ratio is computed asmin gℎ∈G𝑅(gℎ)
max gℎ∈G𝑅(gℎ).
This formula for count-parity, for example, computes the least
common group size divided by the most common group size in the
output cover of an algorithm. A fair algorithm with zero unfairness
should return a fairness ratio of 1, while the smaller the fairness
ratio, the more unfair the algorithm.
All implementations have been run on a server with 24 core CPU
and 128 GB memory with Ubuntu 18 as the OS. The code is publicly
available2.
7.1 Validation
We begin our experiments by validating the problem we study. To
do so, we used the Resume Skills dataset. Using all of its 1986 rows,
we sampled different set of skills 𝑁varying from 10 to 20 out of all
218 available skills as the ground set. We run the brute-force algo-
rithms opt-SC andopt-FSC to find the optimal solutions for the set
cover and the fair set cover, respectively. Next, we used the greedy
algorithm for set cover and EffAllPick to find the approximate fair
set cover. The results are shown in the following.
