B Missing algorithms and proofs from § 6
As a pre-processing step, our algorithm computes 𝑝ℎfor each gℎas
follows. Recall that each 𝑓ℎis rational. Without loss of generality,
we can assume that each 𝑓ℎis represented in its simplest form. Let
𝑝represent the least common multiple (LCM) of the denominators
within the set of fractions 𝑓1,...,𝑓ℎ. Using standard techniques, 𝑝
can be computed efficiently. Set 𝑝ℎ=𝑓ℎ·𝑝for each group 𝑔ℎ∈G.
The performance of our algorithm will have a dependence on 𝑝.
Hence, it is important to observe that 𝑝cannot exceed|S|=𝜇. This
is because in order to satisfy the given fractional constraints, the
size of any cover must be at least 𝑝. However, if 𝑝>𝜇, then this
implies the given instance cannot admit such a fractional cover.
In this section, we need the assumption that every group gℎ∈G
contain at least Ω(log𝑛)𝑓ℎ|𝑋∗|sets. For simplicity, we also assume
that every group contains the same number of sets, denoted by 𝑚.
